{"t":1792602691,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"c4bfa31a-bdcb-49dd-be0d-68f839530ea1","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-21T13:31:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 211331\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n931 AM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nCoastal Flood Advisories issued through Thursday afternoon for\nan extended period of minor tidal flooding.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures are expected\n  through early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures are expected\nthrough early next week.\n\nSurface high pressure continues to build north of the forecast area\nthrough the end of the week, yielding dry conditions, seasonal \ntemperatures, and limited cloud cover. High temperatures will be\nin the 60s to near 70s each day with overnight lows in the 30s to\nmid 40s. \n\nHigh pressure will build southward out of southeastern Canada and \neastern Great Lakes region Saturday through Tuesday. Dry conditions \nare expected each day. There could be some areas of frost Saturday \nmorning. Temperatures will start out chilly Saturday with highs 5 to \n7 degrees below average and highs generally in the upper 50s to \nlower 60s. By Sunday, temperatures will be more seasonable. Monday \nwill encounter highs approaching or reaching the 70 degree mark. By \nTuesday, temperatures may be knocked down to seasonable once again. \nHighs in the middle 60s.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /13Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nVFR conditions continue as surface high pressure builds overhead\nand dry conditions persist. Winds remain primarily out of the\nnorth today before shifting to northwesterly on Thursday. On\nFriday, winds shift to northeasterly. Terminals located near the\nI-95 corridor will gust around 15 knots each afternoon. \nOtherwise, terminals can expect light northerly winds, no \nprecipitation, and VFR conditions. \n\nVFR conditions Saturday through Sunday night. Winds light and \nvariable through the period.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nThe Nor'easter off the Mid-Atlantic coast will deliver a \nprolonged period of SCA conditions (mainly over the \ncentral/lower waters). Small Craft Advisories remain in effect\nand will likely need to be extended. The threat is highest in \nthe lower portions of the waters, with additional Advisories \npossible through the end of the week. Otherwise, dry conditions \nand non-hazardous marine weather is expected. \n\nNo marine hazards expected Saturday through Sunday night. Winds \nlight and variable through the period.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nA Nor'easter will continue to circulate offshore through\nThursday as high pressure builds to our north. This will lead to\npersistent northerly flow over the waters. Elevated tide levels\nare expected through the week as we approach the Full Moon on\nOct 26. Minor tidal flooding is forecast at Annapolis, Dahlgren,\nand Solomons Island where Coastal Flood Advisories have been\nissued through Thursday afternoon's high tide cycle. These are\nlikely to be extended and some other locations may also need\nAdvisories. \n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Coastal Flood Advisory until 6 PM EDT Thursday for MDZ014.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 4 PM EDT Thursday for MDZ016.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 2 PM EDT Thursday for MDZ018.\nVA...Coastal Flood Advisory until 4 PM EDT Thursday for VAZ057.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 8 AM EDT Thursday for ANZ530>534-\n     537>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...AVS/KLW/KRR\nAVIATION...AVS/KLW\nMARINE...AVS/KLW\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792602611,"days":{"2026-10-21":{"hi":65,"lo":44,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-22":{"hi":66,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-23":{"hi":61,"lo":40,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-24":{"